WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.640
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: We turn now to the governance
part of the agenda, item 10, the use of the

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:17.830
NHS England seal. I think despite enormous
interest in this topic, I am going to invite

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:22.370
the Board simply to note it and formally approve
it. Thank you very much.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.680
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: The report reflects the meeting
that was held on 27th July, which I have to

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:40.890
say does seem an age ago, and therefore I
think I will lead that as self-evident and

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:46.070
take any questions. We had a Commissioning
Committee yesterday. We have covered that

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:51.360
we went through the numbers in some considerable
detail, but in line with our rolling programme

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:58.660
of looking at the major business priorities
set out in our business plan, we spent the

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:06.100
majority of the meeting looking at urgent
and emergency care, and self-care. I think

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:13.710
the urgent and emergency care is absolutely
the key to unlocking some of the challenges

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:20.799
that the system faces. We perceive good progress
there, but a tonne of work to do, and incredibly

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:27.399
complicated because it is where all the interfaces
come together. I think we were reassured by

00:01:27.399 --> 00:01:34.909
the team, by the progress and by the plan,
but slightly in awe of the work that needs

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:42.630
to be done to turn that into real, practical
benefit for patients on the ground every day.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:50.099
Self-care is a really important area that
has perhaps been underinvested in. It is an

00:01:50.099 --> 00:01:56.749
important part of our longer-term plan to
manage demand, and I think we came away thinking

00:01:56.749 --> 00:02:02.090
that we had lots of ideas and we needed to
pick a few that would really get on and see

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.390
those come into action.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:12.530
Then the final thing: self-evidently, we spent
time on planning and where we were on the

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.950
translation 
of the STP into the operational plans.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:56.300
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Nothing to add, except in relation
to item 8 on the paper in front of you. Pleased

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:04.909
to say that at our last meeting, following
this one that is reported here, the Dorset

00:03:04.909 --> 00:03:15.349
Clinical Services Review was further considered,
and was subject to some detailed work on how

00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:20.171
some of the finances are presented. It was
agreed for public consultation, and I would

00:03:20.171 --> 00:03:25.430
just like to commend the incredible hard work
that had clearly gone into that from our colleagues

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:27.070
in Dorset.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.300
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: The note for the Board summarises
well the results of our strategic framework

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:44.670
and how that is going into production across
a number of initiatives, which are being implemented

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:50.519
at great pace and great speed. I would just
like to draw the Board’s attention to one

00:03:50.519 --> 00:03:57.209
item that is covered in that note, which is
an important legal success for NHS England

00:03:57.209 --> 00:04:03.030
in the High Court where the Court has now
rejected a claim for judicial review of our

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:09.670
approach to managing hepatitis C. Specifically,
the High Court rejected arguments from the

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:17.530
Hep C Trust, and Gilead, Bristol-Myers Squibb
and AbbVie – as interested parties and drug

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:24.260
companies – against our specialised commissioning
policy. The judge ruled that NHS England was

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:32.850
acting entirely appropriately and reasonably
to phase the implementation of the NICE recommendations

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:41.390
on hep C, and that our monthly run rate approach
is rational and reasonable. We have also been

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:49.330
awarded costs. I have there expanded slightly
on what is in the documented report to the

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:54.410
Board, but the essence of my message is the
same.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:59.060
Malcolm Grant: We note the decision of the
High Court, thank you.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.360
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I do not think there is a
written report from the meeting that was held

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:20.320
last week. I think a couple of things that
I should bring to the Board’s attention.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:27.090
One of the questions that I think we will
need to keep returning to is how we get system-wide

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:36.680
assurance on the integrity of financial control
and operational control, given that we are

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:44.410
increasingly working in partnership, quite
rightly, with NHS Improvement, and therefore

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:50.050
we talked to our internal auditors about how
we can think about that. The conclusion came

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.660
that this is something that is not yet an
issue but will become one, and therefore there

00:05:54.660 --> 00:06:01.320
is an important recognition that, with Paul
and with the team, and with the Internal Audit

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:08.100
team, we need to sit down with the Audit Committee
of NHS Improvement and actually come up with

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:13.100
ways in which we can satisfy ourselves within
our respective remits that we actually cover

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:18.010
the entirety as the system changes. I think
that will be a very important thing for the

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:23.650
audit committees of both organisations over
the year to come. That might turn out to be

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:27.040
a slight surprise to them, because I have
not yet managed to speak to the Chairman of

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:30.970
their Audit Committee to inform him of that
view, and I apologise for that, but I think

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:33.060
it is something that we need to do.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:40.030
The second thing that I would like to highlight
to the Board – and Karen referred to it

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:48.600
before – is that we have an unacceptable
position in relation to PCS, and we have some

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:56.260
challenges with SPS in data processing. On
both of them, I would say there is an incredible

00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:02.930
amount of work gone in to remedying the situation,
within all the parties, including SPS, who

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:08.200
I think have thrown a lot of resource and
capability at trying to improve the situation,

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:16.060
and there is definitely improvement taking
place. I think the Board should first note

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:22.840
and then be aware and reassured that the Audit
Committee will be keeping very close to those

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:27.170
issues and making sure that we are dealing
with them as appropriate, and that there is

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:34.720
no lack of resource or effort on anyone’s
parts to getting those resolved quickly.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:43.890
That would be my comment, other than to welcome
Joanne with huge relief to up the ante on

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:47.050
our audit and risk capability.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.600
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: We should formally accept the to the governance plans.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.420
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: We do formally accept the

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:03.040
changes to the governance arrangements, thank
you very much.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:08.980
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Before drawing the meeting
to a close, I have two items of other business.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:11.060
Does anybody have any other business?

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:16.660
My first is to remind all members of the Board
of our Annual General Meeting: 25th October,

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:21.890
16.30, at the Royal College of Obstetrics
and Gynaecology. By the way, that is simply

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:29.280
the forum, not the topic, as Simon and I are
devising a programme which will allow us to

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:34.430
discuss the Annual Report and Accounts but
also, more generally, the programme of work

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:37.650
that we have been undertaking and which we
are taking forward.

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:43.800
My second item is simply, on behalf of the
Board, to extend thanks again to David. I

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:48.250
apologise to David: I talked him into taking
on, on an interim basis, the chairmanship

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:53.860
of the Audit and Risk Assurance Committee.
Interim turned into something longer than

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:58.630
either he or I had anticipated, but I share
his great delight in welcoming Joanne Shaw,

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:04.700
who will succeed him as from next week as
Chairman of ARAC, and thank David for what

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:08.680
I hope is his last report to the Board as
Chairman of the Audit and Risk Assurance Committee,

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:12.660
although you are remaining a member for as
long as we can manage.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:16.470
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Indeed. I think I should
say, I have been incredibly well served by

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:21.990
Paul and by the team that supports him.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: On that note, may I please

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:28.320
declare the meeting closed with the usual
resolution that we proceed into considering

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.640
private business in part two. Thank you everybody
for attending today, and to my colleagues

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:34.400
for meeting. Thank you.

